# No god files

Large multi-concern files are hard to review and become dump sites for every new feature. Prefer focused types/modules by **use-case**, not by layer name alone.

## Limits (soft / hard)

| | Preferred | Hard stop |
|---|----------:|----------:|
| Infrastructure services, FE feature modules/components | **~400** lines | **~600** lines |

**Exclude from limits:** EF `Migrations/`, generated code, snapshot files. Integration test fixtures may exceed; still prefer splitting when a test file mixes unrelated domains.

## Before adding to an existing file

1. Check line count. If already near/over soft limit, **extract or create a sibling** instead of appending.
2. Split by **use-case** (e.g. member CRUD vs template evolve vs tree read), not by “partial class of the same god.”
3. Prefer injecting the new service into the controller/caller over a forever-growing façade.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — another 200 lines on StructureService
public async Task<...> CreateMemberAsync(...) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — sibling owns the use-case (see StructureLeaderAccountService)
public class StructureMemberService(...) { public async Task<...> CreateMemberAsync(...) { ... } }
```

## With volatility-decomposition

Managers (policy) stay small and pure. Engines stay use-case-sized. A “manager” is not an excuse for a 1k-line service.

## When splitting

- Behavior-preserving moves first; no product changes in the same PR/chunk.
- Register the new type in DI; update call sites; delete dead code from the old file.
- Teach the seam: what moved, what stayed, what would break if left in the god file.
